{
  "configuration_id": "config_024",
  "reason": "Evidence: The current best (config_018, acc=0.619) uses resnet50 as the image encoder, but image-only resnet50 (config_010) achieved only 0.208 accuracy, suggesting very weak image signal. The multimodal boost over RNA-only (0.619 vs 0.591) is modest, likely because resnet50 provides limited complementary information. Question: Does a stronger, ImageNet-pretrained ViT-L encoder yield a substantially richer image representation that, when fused with the multiprofile avgpool RNA signal, produces a larger multimodal gain? This is a clean single-variable change (resnet50 → imagenet_vit_l) from the current best, keeping all other settings (multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool, concat) at their validated-optimal values. Why best next: The image encoder is the largest unexplored source of variance in the multimodal pipeline. If ViT-L substantially improves the image contribution, it unlocks a high-performing multimodal regime and guides all subsequent fusion/aggregation choices. If it does not, we learn that image modality has limited value regardless of encoder, letting us redirect budget toward RNA encoder alternatives (scvi, scgpt) or fusion refinements. Either outcome sharply reduces uncertainty about the most impactful design axis."
}

